WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.092
OPC technologies help industrial applications exchange data across products and vendors.

00:00:06.092 --> 00:00:18.520
Instead of every application implementing every device-specific interface, an OPC layer can expose process values, alarms, events, and other information through a common model.

00:00:18.520 --> 00:00:25.586
OPC Classic refers to older specifications built around Microsoft COM and DCOM technologies.

00:00:25.586 --> 00:00:32.653
These deployments may depend heavily on Windows configuration, service identities, and network permissions.

00:00:32.653 --> 00:00:41.182
Their architecture can be difficult to segment cleanly, so inventory and supported hardening guidance are important.

00:00:41.182 --> 00:00:49.223
OPC Unified Architecture, or OPC UA, is a newer, platform-independent approach with richer information modeling.

00:00:49.223 --> 00:00:53.853
It can support client-server communication and publish-subscribe patterns.

00:00:53.853 --> 00:01:06.281
OPC UA also includes modern security capabilities such as application authentication, user authentication, message signing, and encryption when profiles and endpoints are configured appropriately.

00:01:06.281 --> 00:01:11.398
Available security is not the same as enabled security.

00:01:11.398 --> 00:01:22.851
Administrators must select suitable security policies, restrict anonymous or overly broad access, protect private keys, and manage application certificates and trust lists.

00:01:22.851 --> 00:01:31.867
Expired certificates, copied trust stores, weak fallback endpoints, or inaccurate system time can undermine otherwise strong design.

00:01:31.867 --> 00:01:38.934
OPC servers and gateways often aggregate valuable data and connect multiple architectural levels.

00:01:38.934 --> 00:01:52.824
Treat them as important assets: minimize services, separate management access, monitor configuration and trust changes, limit approved clients, and protect the systems that issue or approve certificates.

00:01:52.824 --> 00:02:01.353
Monitoring should identify expected endpoints, applications, users, subscriptions, and data flows without weakening encryption for convenience.

00:02:01.353 --> 00:02:10.369
When content is protected, metadata, server audit records, certificate events, and endpoint telemetry can still support investigation.

00:02:10.369 --> 00:02:16.461
Secure OPC depends on both protocol features and disciplined lifecycle management.
